---
layout: changelog
title: Patched Node.js runtimes for the October 2026 security release
date: 2026-10-23
tags: security, functions, sites, firewall
---

Functions and sites on Node.js 22, 24 and 26 now run a patched version of Node.js that fixes the vulnerabilities in the [Node.js October 2026 security release](https://nodejs.org/en/blog/vulnerability/october-2026-security-releases).

- **Appwrite Cloud:** functions and sites on these runtimes are updated automatically. You don't need to redeploy.
- **Self-hosted Appwrite:** run these commands from your Appwrite directory. Your functions and sites start on the patched runtimes the next time they run.

```bash
# Pull the patched version of every runtime image on this server
docker images 'openruntimes/*' --format '{{.Repository}}:{{.Tag}}' \
  | grep -v executor | xargs -n1 docker pull
# Stop the executor so it doesn't start new runtimes
docker compose stop openruntimes-executor
# Remove the runtimes still running on the old images
docker ps -aq --filter label=openruntimes-executor | xargs -r docker rm -f
# Start the executor again
docker compose start openruntimes-executor
```

On Appwrite Cloud, the Appwrite firewall also blocks known attack requests for these vulnerabilities before they reach your functions and sites, whichever runtime version they use. This is only a precaution, so move to a patched runtime as soon as possible.

Node.js 16, 18, 19, 20, 21, 23 and 25 are end-of-life, and Node.js doesn't release fixes for them. If a function or site uses one of these versions, change its runtime to Node.js 22, 24 or 26 in its settings and create a new deployment.

